1. Ansible Roles – Apache Deployment Lab
About this lab

In this lab, I learned how to use Ansible Roles to organize my Ansible automation.

In my previous lab, I kept the Apache installation and HTML deployment tasks directly inside a playbook. This time, I moved those tasks into an Apache Role.

I used:

a) WSL Ubuntu as the Ansible control node
b) AWS EC2 Ubuntu as the managed server
c) SSH key authentication
d) Ansible Role
e) Apache2
f) HTML file

2. Lab Setup

Windows
   |
   ↓
WSL Ubuntu
(Ansible)
   |
   | SSH
   ↓
AWS EC2
(Ubuntu)
   |
   ├── Apache2
   └── index.html

3. Create the project

I created a separate directory for this lab:

mkdir ~/ansible-apache-role
cd ~/ansible-apache-role

Project:

ansible-apache-role/

4. Create the inventory

I created an inventory.ini file:

[app_key]
ec2-1 ansible_host=EC2-PUBLIC-IP ansible_user=ubuntu ansible_ssh_private_key_file=~/.ssh/testing.pem

Here:

a) app_key is the inventory group.
b) ec2-1 is the name given to my EC2 server.
c) ansible_host is the public IP of the EC2 instance.
d) ansible_user=ubuntu tells Ansible to use the Ubuntu user.
e) ansible_ssh_private_key_file tells Ansible which .pem key to use.

5. Test the connection

Before running the Role, I checked whether Ansible could connect to the EC2 instance:

ansible app_key -i inventory.ini -m ping

Expecte Result:

ec2-1 | SUCCESS => {
    "changed": false,
    "ping": "pong"
}

This confirmed that Ansible was able to connect to my EC2 instance.

6. Create an Ansible Role

I created the Role using:

ansible-galaxy init roles/apache

This created the standard Role structure.

The important folders for this lab were:

roles/
└── apache/
    ├── files/
    ├── handlers/
    └── tasks/

7. Add the HTML file

I placed my index.html inside the Role's files directory:

roles/apache/files/index.html

The files directory is used for files that need to be copied to the managed server.

8. Create the Role tasks

I edited:

roles/apache/tasks/main.yml

and added:

---
- name: Install Apache
  ansible.builtin.apt:
    name: apache2
    state: present
    update_cache: yes

- name: Copy index.html
  ansible.builtin.copy:
    src: index.html
    dest: /var/www/html/index.html
    owner: root
    group: root
    mode: '0644'

What these tasks do

The first task installs Apache2:

name: apache2
state: present

The second task copies my HTML file:

src: index.html

Ansible automatically looks for this file inside:

roles/apache/files/

It copies the file to:

/var/www/html/index.html

on the EC2 server.

8. Create the handler

I created a handler in:

roles/apache/handlers/main.yml

---
- name: Restart Apache
  ansible.builtin.service:
    name: apache2
    state: restarted

A handler is a special task that runs when another task tells it to run.

I added this to the copy task:

notify: Restart Apache

So the task became:

- name: Copy index.html
  ansible.builtin.copy:
    src: index.html
    dest: /var/www/html/index.html
    owner: root
    group: root
    mode: '0644'
  notify: Restart Apache

If the HTML file is changed, Ansible will notify the handler and restart Apache.

9. Create the main playbook

I created:

site.yaml

The playbook is very small because the actual work is inside the Role.

---
- name: Deploy Apache Web Server
  hosts: app_key
  become: true

  roles:
    - apache

10. Here:

roles:
  - apache

tells Ansible:

Use the apache Role for this server.

10. Project structure

After completing the setup, my project looked like this:

ansible-apache-role/
│
├── inventory.ini
├── site.yaml
│
└── roles/
    └── apache/
        ├── files/
        │   └── index.html
        │
        ├── handlers/
        │   └── main.yml
        │
        └── tasks/
            └── main.yml

11. Check the playbook

Before running it, I checked the YAML syntax:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yaml --syntax-check

Expected:

playbook: site.yaml

12. Run the Role

I ran:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yaml

Then Ansible perform this operation 

WSL
 ↓
Read site.yaml
 ↓
Call apache Role
 ↓
Install Apache
 ↓
Copy index.html
 ↓
Restart Apache if required
 ↓
EC2 server

13. Verify Apache

I checked whether Apache was running:

ansible app_key -i inventory.ini -m shell -a "systemctl is-active apache2"

Expected:

active

I also checked whether the HTML file was copied:

ansible app_key -i inventory.ini -m shell -a "ls -l /var/www/html/index.html"

14. Verify the website

I opened the EC2 public IP in my browser:

http://EC2-PUBLIC-IP

The HTML page was displayed successfully.

This confirmed that the Role had installed Apache and deployed the webpage correctly.

15. Idempotency test

I ran the same playbook again:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yaml

Since Apache was already installed and the HTML file had not changed, Ansible did not make unnecessary changes.

This helped me understand idempotency in Ansible.

15. What I learned

Through this lab, I learned:

a) What an Ansible Role is
b) How to create a Role using ansible-galaxy
c) How tasks/main.yml works
d) How the files directory is used
e) How to use handlers
f) How to call a Role from a playbook
g) How to organize Ansible projects
h) How idempotency works
i) How to deploy Apache using a Role
